**The two disputed sentences**

For item 6, the decisive evidence is in [r20’s gapped sessions](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/crimewidthkill/final529r20_20260930/crime_width_probe.r20.py:1648). Both write variants plant an **`sw`**, with `at` pointing to TIME and `t0=0x0a0b0c0d` (:1651–1653 and :1689–1691). The debugger’s `put w` commands plant instruction words; the encoded guest instruction is what determines the device access width.

At that address, the [device’s four-byte arm](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c:282) stores into the **upper** word. Only afterward does TIME dispatch call the update. A positive delta truncates the register; a zero delta does not.

Thus a suitable correction is:

> The gapped write sessions seed TIME’s upper word. The update may immediately truncate it; these rows do not establish a persistent nonzero upper word at a refused-read observation.

This establishes a false record. It does **not** establish a new measured coverage escape.

For item 8, grok’s inference goes too far. Device:377 tests the aggregate residue against zero; it does not separately mask or compare bit 0 as a case. The [carrier’s explicit definition of “TESTS”](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/CLAUDE.md:1474) preserves that distinction. Existing rows exercising bit 0 do not bring every bit-conditioned edit into the required floor. Retain clause (e), with “not individually tested or cleared.”

